The way I envision it is that the tags will be listed in #enhance menu. So 
if someone enhances the "piercing" skill, then all weapons having that tag 
will become more effective in their hands.

If a skill tree is used instead of the flat list currently implemented, then 
complexity will obviously increase.
E.g. Lets say there is a top level tag named "blade" which encompasses all 
blade weapons. Under this tag there is another tier of tags which provides 
specialization like "long blade" and "short blade", i.e. a person is fairly 
skilled in using blade weapons in general and now wants to specialize in a 
particular type of blade weapon, much like the way it happens in real life.

The level of expertise in any given skill, "unskilled", "skilled", "expert" 
etc. will be maintained. In the above example, if someone wants to advance 
the "long blade" skill, that should only be allowed when they are already at 
some skill level in "blade" weapons in general.
The system might follow either of the following approaches:
 * require a player to max the general skill first and only then allow 
advancing the specific sub-skills
 * allow advancing a specific sub-skill at any time but not more that the 
level of the general skill

Every weapon might thus have a base modifier which factors in the general, 
top-level skill level and another modifier which accounts for the level of 
any specialized skills under that top-level skill. These pair of modifiers 
could then be calculated on a per tag basis for every weapon and the final 
result might be a summation of all modifiers.

Spending of skill points might again mirror the real life. Attaining around 
about average level of skill in a particular trade/craft requires less time 
compared to honing and polishing a specialized aspect therin. So the rate of 
progress in the general, top-level skills could be faster than that in the 
specializations. Similarly, the effort required in leveling up general 
skills could be less than that for specilized sub-skills.

You raised a good point about the number of tags per weapon, I had not 
considered that. I guess, all weapons should be balanced to have about the 
same number of tags. Say a particular weapon "XYZ" has one or two more tags 
than the average number of tags per weapon in the game but unless the player 
has enhanced those skills, that would not mean much. Skill points must be 
spent first to realize that potential and this along with adjustment of the 
chance of that weapon being generated could balance things out.
In worst case, "XYZ" might become the new "Grayswandir" ;)


